er with, but I can't. That will be very weak. This forces me to burn cards, and I’m hoping to clear with this play. With a triplet and a single, my hand has many moves and lots of openings. I'm going to take the initiative and force them to respond.\n\n**So, I'm going with the 9-9-9 and the 3.  Let's see what happens.**\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The current hand consists of a triplet (999), two strong pairs (KK, AA), a high single (2), and several weak singles (3, 6, 8, Q). Since it is a Free Play, the goal is to play a combination that clears weak cards and maintains control or forces high cards from opponents. The triplet of 9s is a solid middle-rank combination. Because there are multiple weak singles (3, 6, 8), attaching the lowest single (3) to the triplet is the most efficient move. This play (9-9-9-3) removes the weakest card and challenges opponents to respond with a triplet of 10s or better (noting that KKK and AAA are impossible as I hold pairs of Kings and Aces). This strategy preserves the strong pairs and the 2 for defense.\n\nReason: Playing the triplet with an attached single (999+3) efficiently removes the lowest trash card and reduces the total number of turns required to finish. It leverages the triplet structure to force out opponent strength while holding back high pairs (KK, AA) and the 2 for later control.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦3", "♠9", "♣9", "♦9"]}'}
